talk(692,'1986-11-03','G. I. Olshanskii','P001313-P001316','Spherical functions behind principal-minor integrals',r'The actual exposition begins on P001314 and ends in the upper part of P001315. Olshanskii explains the Gelfand--Naimark formula through highest-weight characters and spherical functions, with HCIZ as a limiting form. Gelfand asks which other integrals admit such an interpretation.')
item(692,'P001314','theorem','The principal-minor spherical integral in full normalization',r'The integrand is a product of powers of leading principal minors of \(URU^*\); the answer has alternating powers of eigenvalues and parameter differences.',r'Let \(r_i>0\), \(R=\operatorname{diag}(r_i)\), and \(a_j=s_j+N-j\). For normalized Haar measure, \[\int_{U(N)}\prod_{k=1}^N\Delta_k(URU^*)^{s_k-s_{k+1}}dU=\frac{\bigl(\prod_{k=1}^{N-1}k!\bigr)\det[r_i^{a_j}]}{\Delta(r)\prod_{i<j}(a_i-a_j)},\quad s_{N+1}=0.\] Both eigenvalue and parameter collisions have removable limits; all complex powers use positive bases.',r'\citet[Prop. 2.1, citing Gelfand--Naimark, II.9.3]{faraut2014rayleigh} gives this formula with proof. The original theorem predates 1986 by decades.',['faraut2014rayleigh'],reconstruction=r'The exact normalization, exponent differences, and Weyl shifts reconstruct the abbreviated formula.')
item(692,'P001314-P001315','method','Contraction from spherical functions to HCIZ',r'The notes say formula (2), the exponential orbital integral, follows from the first formula.',r'With fixed Hermitian \(X\), let \(R_\tau=I+X/\tau>0\) for sufficiently large \(\tau\), and replace the spherical parameters by \(\tau s\). The principal-minor powers tend to an exponential whose exponent telescopes to \(\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{diag}(s)UXU^*)\). Compactness permits passage under the integral; the determinant side has the corresponding confluent limit.',r'\citet[Sec. 2, after Prop. 2.2]{faraut2014rayleigh} states precisely this Mehler--Heine-type limit. It explains the relation asserted in the notebook without claiming that the limit calculation was written there.',['faraut2014rayleigh'],reconstruction=r'The scaling and positivity range are editorial reconstruction of the indicated implication.',publication_match='partial')
item(692,'P001315','construction','Characters, highest weights, and spherical matrix coefficients',r'For integral parameters the formula is related to Weyl\textquotesingle s character formula; arbitrary complex parameters are interpreted through representations of \(\operatorname{GL}_N(\mathbb C)\).',r'Dominant integral highest weights give Schur characters, expressed by the alternant divided by a Vandermonde. Averaging a highest-weight coefficient gives a normalized spherical function on \(\operatorname{GL}_N(\mathbb C)/U(N)\). Complex continuation defines the spherical family, but arbitrary complex inducing parameters do not give unitary representations.',r'\citet[Sec. 2]{faraut2014rayleigh} verifies the spherical formula; its shifts explain the character relation. Unitarity must be checked separately and is discussed for \(\operatorname{GL}_N\) in record 697.',['faraut2014rayleigh'],reconstruction=r'The distinction between polynomial highest weights, analytic continuation, and the unitary parameter range is supplied.',publication_match='partial')
item(692,'P001315','question','Which integrals are evaluations of spherical functions?',r'Gelfand asks which other integrals can be evaluated in this representation-theoretic way.',r'The note specifies neither a class of integrands nor an admissible group and representation construction. A precise finite family is given by principal-minor averages and their orbital-integral limits, but a classification of all integrals having some representation-theoretic realization is not a defined question.',r'The adjacent formulas are established examples; \citet[Secs. 2--3]{faraut2014rayleigh} explains their common harmonic-analytic origin. No exact later theorem is matched to the unrestricted wording.',['faraut2014rayleigh'],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',confidence='low',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 this is a research-program remark, not an identifiable unsolved problem.')
save()

talk(693,'1986-11-03','V. G. Drinfeld','P001314-P001320','Quantizing KdV and the second Gelfand--Dickey bracket',r'The section actually runs from the lower part of P001315 through P001318. It distinguishes the compatible KdV brackets, their free-field and Miura realizations, higher-order operators, and quantum commuting integrals. Gelfand and Feigin intervene. Three final questions concern quantization of KdV integrals, their spectrum in irreducible Virasoro representations, and general second-bracket quantization. The spectral question receives a bounded level-two development in this chapter; P001319 instead begins Beilinson on 10 November.')
item(693,'P001315','construction','The two compatible Poisson structures of KdV',r'The notes write \(P_1=-2\partial\) and \(P_2=-\frac12\partial^3-u\partial-\partial u\), then ask about quantization.',r'On local differential-polynomial functionals of a smooth periodic field \(u\), or with boundary conditions eliminating integration-by-parts terms, \(P_1,P_2\) define compatible Hamiltonian operators. Every constant linear combination is Poisson. Appropriate Hamiltonians and rescaling of \(u,x,t\) give the KdV hierarchy; coefficients cannot be compared across conventions without the rescaling.',r'\citet[Secs. 2--3]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} surveys the classical structures with proofs, following the Gelfand--Dickey theory. The Russian original appeared in 1984, the English translation in 1985.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],reconstruction=r'The class of functionals and boundary conditions are supplied; the notebook does not fix a functional-analytic phase space.')
item(693,'P001315','question','A simultaneous quantization with two Planck constants',r'Two compatible brackets suggest two Planck constants.',r'The notebook does not specify a flat two-parameter associative algebra, a representation, or which classical pencil it should recover. In the displayed classical pencil, shifting \(u\) by a constant changes \(P_2\) by a multiple of \(\partial\), so independence of two quantum parameters is not automatic.',r'The classical pencil in \citet{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} and the second-structure quantum KdV construction in \citet{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable} are relevant, but neither is identified with this unspecified simultaneous-quantization proposal.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie','bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],cls='F',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'The obstruction is a missing definition, not evidence of an undeveloped mathematical conjecture.',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 no definite two-parameter assertion is recoverable from this sentence.')
item(693,'P001315','remark','Nonlinear quantization and perturbing a measure',r'Gelfand says that genuinely nonlinear quantization is not understood, mentions quantum groups and a perturbation of a measure; Drinfeld contrasts approaches associated with Faddeev and Albeverio--Høegh-Krohn.',r'No measure space, perturbing functional, regularization, or proposed equivalence is stated. The passage records methodological preferences about quantization, not a theorem comparing the named constructions.',r'The surrounding quantum-KdV problem acquired concrete algebraic constructions, but the broad methodological comparison has no exact mathematical formulation here.',['bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand and V. G. Drinfeld',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',confidence='low',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 this remains an under-specified programmatic remark, not a separate open problem.')
item(693,'P001316','construction','The second bracket as the Virasoro Lie--Poisson structure',r'Fourier expansion of the second bracket yields the Virasoro algebra and Kirillov\textquotesingle s orbit picture.',r'The affine hyperplane of fixed central coordinate in the dual Virasoro algebra has a Lie--Poisson bracket of the form \(a\partial^3+2u\partial+u^{\prime}\), up to sign and scale. Quantization replaces the Fourier-mode Poisson brackets by Virasoro commutators. This identifies the algebra of fields; it does not by itself order the nonlinear Hamiltonians.',r'\citet[Sec. 2]{ovsienkokhesin1987super} recalls the already known Virasoro Lie--Poisson identification; \citet[Eq. (2), (11)--(14)]{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable} separates it from the quantum charges.',['ovsienkokhesin1987super','bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],reconstruction=r'The chapter fixes the standard convention \([L_m,L_n]=(m-n)L_{m+n}+c(m^3-m)\delta_{m+n,0}/12\); the notebook uses a different index/sign convention.')
item(693,'P001316','theorem','Classical local commuting KdV Hamiltonians',r'The integrals \(F_n\) are said to commute, and a quantum analogue is attributed to Zamolodchikov.',r'For a scalar second-order Lax operator, residues of appropriate odd half-integer powers produce local functionals forming a Poisson-commutative hierarchy. They are defined modulo total derivatives; periodicity or decay makes their integrals well defined. Classical commutativity does not imply commutativity after an arbitrary ordering of modes.',r'\citet[Sec. 2]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} gives the classical residue construction. The quantum question is separated as GP-1986-32.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],reconstruction=r'The formal pseudodifferential convention and total-derivative quotient are supplied.')
item(693,'P001316; P001318','problem','Quantization of the full family of local KdV integrals',r'The closing question 1(a) asks to quantize the KdV integrals; an earlier line says Zamolodchikov has quantum analogues.',r'Construct local normally ordered Virasoro charges \(I_{2k-1}\) deforming the classical KdV Hamiltonians, with \([I_{2k-1},I_{2l-1}]=0\), rather than merely quantizing the underlying bracket. The construction is naturally formal or generic in the deformation parameter; specialized representations and convergence need separate treatment.',r'\citet[Thm. 4.7.3, with the generic-parameter hypotheses of Sec. 4.7.1]{feiginfrenkel1995integrals} proves deformation and commutativity in the relevant affine \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) case (1993 circulation; 1995 publication). \citet[Eq. (11) and quantum monodromy construction]{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable} gives explicit initial charges and transfer operators (1994 circulation; 1996 publication).',['feiginfrenkel1995integrals','bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],cls='B',problem='GP-1986-32',literal_correctness='correct',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the algebraic construction of commuting quantum KdV charges is established. Their simultaneous spectral classification is a separate, only partly resolved problem.',reconstruction=r'The normalization and formal/generic meaning of quantization are supplied. No assertion that an arbitrary ordering works is attributed to the notes.')
item(693,'P001316','construction','Miura transformation and the Heisenberg Poisson field',r'The substitution \(u=v^{\prime}-v^2\) is called Poisson for the second Gelfand--Dickey bracket, with a derivative-of-delta bracket on \(v\).',r'For the displayed normalization \(P_2=-\frac12\partial^3-u\partial-\partial u\), take \(P_v=\frac12\partial\). The differential of the Miura map is \(D=\partial-2v\), and \(DP_vD^*=P_2\). If \(P_v=\partial\), the pushforward is \(2P_2\). This is an identity of formal Hamiltonian differential operators; global surjectivity on smooth periodic fields is not asserted.',r'\citet[Sec. 3.7]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} treats the Miura Poisson realization. The factor depends on the bracket normalization, which the notebook does not keep consistently fixed.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],reconstruction=r'The explicit factor \(1/2\) is an editorial normalization check, not a claim that the handwritten Heisenberg coefficient had that value.',development='annotated')
item(693,'P001316','construction','Normal-ordered free-boson realization of Virasoro',r'The modes of the Heisenberg field are used to form quadratic \(L_n\); the displayed sum has only \(p>q\).',r'With \([a_m,a_n]=m\delta_{m+n,0}\), the normally ordered modes \(L_n=\frac12\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}:a_{n-k}a_k:\) act on a Heisenberg Fock module and satisfy Virasoro at \(c=1\). A background-charge derivative changes the central charge. Omitting the diagonal term when \(n=2k\), or omitting normal ordering and zero-mode data, does not give the same operator.',r'\citet[Secs. 4.2 and 4.4]{feiginfrenkel1995integrals} develops the Heisenberg vertex-algebra realization; \citet[Eq. (31)]{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable} uses the corresponding quantum Miura field. The construction itself predates the report.',['feiginfrenkel1995integrals','bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The conventional \(c=1\) realization is stated exactly; the notebook\textquotesingle s unrestricted sum is replaced by its required completion and ordering.')
item(693,'P001317','construction','Higher-order scalar operators and Miura factorization',r'\(L=\partial^n+u_{n-2}\partial^{n-2}+\cdots+u_0=(\partial+v_1)\cdots(\partial+v_n)\), with \(\sum v_i=0\), is associated with \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\), Wilson, Drinfeld, and Sokolov.',r'Expanding the ordered product defines a differential-polynomial map from \(n\) first-order fields with sum zero to the coefficients of a monic scalar operator without its \(\partial^{n-1}\) term. The ordering matters because \(\partial f=f\partial+f^{\prime}\). Reversing the map may require a differential-field extension or local solutions; it is not an unconditional global factorization over the original smooth periodic coefficient ring.',r'\citet[Sec. 3.7, Prop. 3.26]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie} supplies the generalized Miura setting. The reference to Picard--Vessiot theory in the notes is consistent with the field-extension qualification.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],reconstruction=r'The forward construction is distinguished from the stronger, generally obstructed global inverse statement.')
item(693,'P001317','claim','The nonlinear W3 algebra and higher quantum Miura fields',r'The \(n=3\) second bracket gives a nonlinear algebra; Fateev, Zamolodchikov, and a Feigin report in April 1986 are mentioned.',r'The principal quantum \(W_3\) algebra has generating fields of spins two and three whose operator products close nonlinearly through normally ordered composites. It is the \(\mathfrak{sl}_3\) instance of quantum Drinfeld--Sokolov reduction. This is different from a finite-dimensional Lie algebra with bilinear structure constants.',r'\citet{fateevzamolodchikov1987z3} publishes the matching spin-three symmetry; \citet{feiginfrenkel1990quantization} gives the general \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\) BRST interpretation. The precise content of Feigin\textquotesingle s April conference remark is not separately recoverable.',['fateevzamolodchikov1987z3','feiginfrenkel1990quantization'],cls='B',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The \(W_3\) identification is strong, but the notes do not contain its full operator-product coefficients.',confidence='high')
item(693,'P001317','remark','Sklyanin and quantum KdV on a finite interval',r'One line mentions Sklyanin\textquotesingle s quantum KdV on a finite interval with boundary conditions.',r'No endpoint conditions, Hamiltonian, reflection matrix, or representation are specified. Periodic quantum KdV cannot simply be substituted for this boundary problem.',r'The quantum inverse-scattering context is documented by \citet{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable}, but it is not an exact publication match for the unrecorded boundary conditions. The occurrence remains unidentified.',['bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'],cls='F',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',confidence='low',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 no precise interval problem is determined by the surviving sentence.')
item(693,'P001317-P001318','method','The sweep method as a Riccati or Miura transformation',r'Gelfand considers \(y^{\prime\prime}-a(x)y=0\), propagates \(y^{\prime}+\alpha y=0\), and writes \(\alpha^{\prime}-\alpha^2=0\) with an exclamation mark.',r'On an interval where a chosen solution does not vanish, \(\alpha=-y^{\prime}/y\) satisfies \(\alpha^{\prime}-\alpha^2=-a\). Conversely this Riccati equation yields the factorization \(\partial^2-a=(\partial-\alpha)(\partial+\alpha)\). Zeros of \(y\) create Riccati poles; propagation must then change charts or use a matrix first-order system.',r'The factorization mechanism is the scalar Miura construction discussed in \citet[Sec. 3.7]{drinfeldsokolov1985lie}. \ours\ The corrected sign and nonzero right-hand side follow directly by differentiating \(-y^{\prime}/y\); the scan confirms that zero is the actual notebook reading.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The omitted forcing \(-a\), zero-free interval, and chart limitation are supplied.')
item(693,'P001318','remark','Higher-order and elliptic boundary propagation',r'Gelfand says the sweep method also works in higher order and sketches shrinking a contour for \(\Delta u=0\), mentioning Keldysh.',r'Higher-order scalar ODEs can be converted to first-order systems and boundary subspaces propagated by their fundamental solution. For elliptic PDEs, contour propagation leads to boundary operator families and is not the same finite-dimensional Riccati equation. The note gives no domain, regularity, solvability, or operator formula for the PDE version.',r'The ODE part is standard and compatible with the factorization context of \citet{drinfeldsokolov1985lie}; no exact Keldysh theorem is identified with the elliptic sketch.',['drinfeldsokolov1985lie'],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',confidence='medium',reconstruction=r'The finite-dimensional ODE content is separated from the unidentified PDE claim.',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the ODE propagation mechanism is established; this specific elliptic-boundary assertion lacks sufficient data for assessment.')
item(693,'P001318','construction','Feigin: invariant forms, Casimirs, and loop algebras',r'Feigin writes the quadratic Casimir \(\sum e_i e^i\), loop \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\), and scalar products obtained from coefficients of \(\operatorname{tr}(uv)\); higher Casimirs are linked to Gelfand--Dickey Hamiltonians.',r'For a finite-dimensional Lie algebra with nondegenerate invariant symmetric form, dual bases define a central quadratic Casimir in its enveloping algebra. On the loop algebra, coefficient or residue pairings \(\operatorname{Res}\operatorname{tr}(u(z)v(z))z^m dz\) are invariant. Higher invariant polynomials, after Hamiltonian reduction, give classical \(W\)-fields; quantum versions require normal ordering and corrections.',r'\citet[Secs. 2.4 and 4.6]{feiginfrenkel1995integrals} explains the classical and quantum reductions. The bare Casimir construction is old; the exact unpublished formula allegedly given in April is not filled in.',['feiginfrenkel1995integrals'],speaker='B. L. Feigin',publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'Nondegeneracy, invariance, and the distinction between central finite-dimensional Casimirs and reduced local fields are supplied.')
item(693,'P001318','problem','Spectrum of quantum KdV charges on irreducible Virasoro modules',r'Question 1(b) asks to calculate the spectrum in irreducible representations of the Virasoro algebra, immediately after quantizing the KdV integrals.',r'Fix \(c,h\in\mathbb C\), the irreducible highest-weight module \(L(c,h)\), and normalized commuting local KdV charges. Determine their joint generalized eigenspaces and nilpotent actions grade by grade, and a complete all-grade parameterization where available. An ordinary eigenbasis requires semisimplicity, which is not automatic outside a positive unitary setting.',r'\citet[Eq. (11)]{bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable} supplies explicit charges and a spectral program. The opened v1 of \citet[Defs. 6.5--6.6, Prop. 6.7, Thm. 6.8, Sec. 7]{masoeroruzza2026odeim} gives a 2026 preprint completeness theorem at \(c=-2\), \(h=(\beta^2-1)/8\), \(\beta\notin\mathbb Z\), for admissible QQ solutions modulo entire-function gauge, and diagonalizes the first three charges in its free-field realization. This does not settle arbitrary \(c,h\).',['bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable','masoeroruzza2026odeim'],cls='C',problem='GP-1986-33',development='developed in this chapter',literal_correctness='correct',publication_match='partial',worth=r'Worth a bounded development: exact low-grade matrices and singular-vector quotient checks reveal semisimplicity obstructions without claiming all-grade completeness.',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the general formulation remains only partly resolved. \oursComputed\ This chapter computes the level-two charge and proves a nonunitary Jordan block at \(c=1,h=-1/32\) survives in the irreducible quotient; the result is explicitly a bounded calculation, with no historical novelty claim.',reconstruction=r'The spectrum is read as the spectrum of the quantum KdV family, not merely \(L_0\). The highest-weight restriction and generalized-eigenspace target are modern specifications absent from the notebook.')
item(693,'P001318','problem','Quantization of the general second Gelfand--Dickey bracket',r'Question 2 asks for quantization of the second Gelfand--Dickey bracket in the general case.',r'For monic scalar differential operators of order \(n\), with the \(\partial^{n-1}\) coefficient removed, construct a vertex or completed mode algebra deforming the second Adler--Gelfand--Dickey Poisson algebra. In the principal \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\) setting this is the quantum \(W_n\) algebra; the precise reduction and deformation parameter must be specified.',r'\citet{feiginfrenkel1990quantization} identifies quantum Drinfeld--Sokolov reduction of affine \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\) with \(W_n\). \citet[Thm. 4.6.9 and Sec. 4.6.10]{feiginfrenkel1995integrals} proves the more general simple-Lie-algebra construction and explains its BRST relation.',['feiginfrenkel1990quantization','feiginfrenkel1995integrals'],cls='B',problem='GP-1986-34',literal_correctness='correct',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the algebraic quantization in the principal scalar-operator setting is established. This statement does not claim that every global analytic boundary-value problem is thereby solved.',reconstruction=r'The scalar principal-reduction setting is the strongest explicit setting supported by the preceding \(\mathfrak{sl}_n\) discussion.')
problem('GP-1986-32','Commuting quantizations of local KdV Hamiltonians',r'Construct commuting normally ordered deformations of the complete family of classical local KdV integrals.',['1986-693.6'],r'As of 12 November 2026 solved as an algebraic/formal and generic-parameter construction; spectral classification and exceptional representation specializations are distinct questions.',['feiginfrenkel1995integrals','bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable'])
problem('GP-1986-33','Quantum KdV spectrum in irreducible Virasoro representations',r'For fixed central charge and highest weight, determine the joint spectral and Jordan data of the commuting quantum KdV charges on the irreducible highest-weight module.',['1986-693.15'],r'As of 12 November 2026 partly solved, with explicit constructions and special-regime results. The opened 2026 Masoero--Ruzza v1 proves a particular c=-2 admissible-QQ completeness statement for nonintegral momentum; it is not an arbitrary-parameter theorem. The chapter provides a checked level-two Jordan obstruction in a nonunitary irreducible quotient.',['bazhanovlukyanovzamolodchikov1996integrable','masoeroruzza2026odeim'])
problem('GP-1986-34','Quantization of second Adler--Gelfand--Dickey structures',r'Construct a quantum algebra deforming the second Gelfand--Dickey bracket on monic scalar operators of arbitrary order, in the principal Drinfeld--Sokolov setting.',['1986-693.16'],r'As of 12 November 2026 the quantum W-algebra/BRST construction resolves the algebraic problem, with precise generic-parameter identifications in the cited sources.',['feiginfrenkel1990quantization','feiginfrenkel1995integrals'])
corr('P001316','Quadratic oscillator modes','Sum only over p>q','Include the diagonal half-square when p=q, and specify normal ordering and zero modes.','Russian transcription explicitly confirms strict p>q; compare standard free-boson Virasoro modes and BLZ Eq. (31).')
corr('P001318','Riccati sweep equation','alpha prime - alpha squared = 0','alpha prime - alpha squared = -a(x), for y prime + alpha y=0 and y double prime-a y=0.','Original scan viewed; Russian source explicitly confirms the zero and exclamation mark. Direct differentiation provides the stated editorial repair.')
