talk(694,'1986-11-10','A. A. Beilinson','','Virasoro uniformization and differential equations on moduli of curves',r'The catalogue gives no page range, but the original P001319 heading clearly names Beilinson and 10 November 1986. The coherent continuation P001319--P001321 supplies surviving notes: formal-coordinate uniformization of pointed curves, determinant-line twists, and differential equations obtained from singular Virasoro vectors. This attribution is an explicit editorial recovery from the manuscript; the empty catalogue page field is retained. The note credits Kontsevich and Beilinson--Schechtman independently and refers to Belavin through Drinfeld.')
item(694,'P001319','construction','The universal pointed curve and its dimensions','The diagram \\(X\\to M\\) has a genus-\\(g\\) curve as fiber; the displayed dimension is \\(6g-6\\).','For \\(g\\ge2\\), the smooth moduli stack \\(\\mathcal M_g\\) has complex dimension \\(3g-3\\), hence real dimension \\(6g-6\\). Its universal curve is \\(\\mathcal M_{g,1}\\to\\mathcal M_g\\), with total complex dimension \\(3g-2\\). On a coarse moduli space a universal family need not exist without a level structure or stack interpretation.',r'This is classical moduli theory, used explicitly in \citet[Sec. 1.1 and Sec. 4.2]{benzvifrenkel2004geometric}. The notebook dimension is correct only as a real dimension of the base.',['benzvifrenkel2004geometric'],reconstruction=r'The genus restriction, complex versus real dimension, and stack interpretation are supplied.',development='annotated')
item(694,'P001319','construction','Cotangent and Hodge determinant line twists','For integers \\(j,c\\), the fiber at \\((K,x)\\) is \\((\\Omega^1_{K,x})^{\\otimes j}\\otimes[\\det\\Omega^1(K)]^c\\).','On \\(\\mathcal M_{g,1}\\), write \\(\\psi\\) for the cotangent line at the marked point and \\(\\lambda=\\det\\pi_*\\omega_\\pi\\) for the Hodge line pulled back from \\(\\mathcal M_g\\). The displayed bundle is \\(\\psi^{\\otimes j}\\otimes\\lambda^{\\otimes c}\\), with negative integral powers interpreted as duals. A complex central parameter generally defines a twist of differential operators, not an ordinary fractional tensor power of a line bundle.',r'\citet{beilinsonschechtman1988determinant}, received 12 February 1988 and published that December, develops determinant bundles and Virasoro action. \citet[Sec. 4.2]{benzvifrenkel2004geometric} treats both actual line bundles and arbitrary complex twists.',['beilinsonschechtman1988determinant','benzvifrenkel2004geometric'],reconstruction='The notation \\(\\Omega^1(K)\\) is interpreted as the finite-dimensional space of global holomorphic differentials. The exponent \\(c\\) is not identified numerically with a Virasoro central charge without fixing the central-extension convention.',publication_match='partial')
item(694,'P001319-P001320','theorem','Formal-coordinate uniformization and the Virasoro lift','The space \\(\\widehat X\\ni(K,x,q)\\) carries an action of formal vector fields; \\(t\\mathbb C[[t]]\\partial_t\\) changes the coordinate and the remaining modes vary the curve.','The formal-coordinate torsor over \\(\\mathcal M_{g,1}\\) has a transitive infinitesimal action of \\(\\operatorname{Der}\\mathbb C((t))\\), compatible with the coordinate-change group \\(\\operatorname{Aut}\\mathbb C[[t]]\\). The central generator acts trivially on the base; the lift to determinant-line differential operators is governed by the Virasoro central extension. This is an infinitesimal algebraic statement, not a global action of an unspecified infinite-dimensional Lie group.',r'The directly corresponding publication is \citet{beilinsonschechtman1988determinant}. The action and its determinant-line lift are stated and used in \citet[Sec. 1.1 and Prop. 4.2.3]{benzvifrenkel2004geometric}, first circulated January 2003. The precise independent Kontsevich priority is recorded from the notebook, without an identified separate manuscript.',['beilinsonschechtman1988determinant','benzvifrenkel2004geometric'],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The distinction between the centerless action on coordinates and the central lift on a line twist makes the notebook shorthand precise.')
item(694,'P001320','method','Kodaira--Spencer tangent space through punctured-curve vector fields','The tangent quotient is written as \\(\\mathit{Vir}/\\Phi_q(\\mathcal T(K\\backslash X))\\), then with the point \\(x\\) in the denominator; two horizontal lifts are compared.','For a smooth pointed curve \\(C,p\\) with formal coordinate \\(q\\), the tangent space to the coordinate torsor is the quotient \\(\\operatorname{Der}\\mathbb C((q))/H^0(C\\setminus\\{p\\},T_C)\\), where algebraic vector fields have finite-order poles at \\(p\\) and are expanded in \\(q\\). Comparing trivializations of an infinitesimal deformation on the formal disc and punctured complement gives the quotient; changing the complement lift adds a global punctured-curve vector field.','\\citet[Sec. 4.2]{benzvifrenkel2004geometric} gives the uniformization and stabilizer formulation. The notebook itself explains the two-lift mechanism. The capital \\(X\\) in the first denominator is incompatible with the adjoining definitions.',['benzvifrenkel2004geometric'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The correct point removal, algebraic rather than arbitrary holomorphic vector fields, and finite Laurent pole condition are supplied.')
item(694,'P001321','method','Singular Virasoro vectors produce canonical differential operators','A reducible Verma module \\(M_{(c,g)}\\) contains a highest-weight vector \\(v^{\\prime}=L(v)\\); applying \\(L\\in U(\\mathit{Vir})\\) to a lifted section gives an equation between line twists.',r'Given a singular vector of definite grade in a Virasoro highest-weight module, localization on the formal-coordinate torsor, followed by equivariant descent, produces a differential operator between the appropriate twisted sheaves. Contragredient conventions determine the direction and weight shift. The construction requires compatibility with the coordinate-change action; a section is represented by an equivariant function, not literally by a single vector of a fixed Verma module.',r'The genus-zero null-vector mechanism occurs in \citet{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}. Its moduli-space globalization belongs to \citet{beilinsonschechtman1988determinant}; \citet[Secs. 2.2--2.4 and 4.2]{benzvifrenkel2004geometric} gives a precise localization framework.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','beilinsonschechtman1988determinant','benzvifrenkel2004geometric'],cls='A',publication_match='partial',reconstruction='The highest weight written \\(g\\) is renamed \\(h\\) when needed to avoid confusing it with curve genus. No unrecorded central-charge-to-Hodge-power formula is imposed.')
item(694,'P001321','claim','Finite-rank solution local systems require a specified conformal-block sector',r'The closing sentence says that solutions of the singular-vector equations form a finite-rank local system.',r'A system of differential equations gives a finite-rank local system on a smooth locus when its corresponding differential module is an integrable connection of finite rank there. Mere reducibility of one arbitrary Virasoro Verma module does not establish this property. To identify the intended conformal-block theorem one needs the representations, insertions, quotient relations, and finiteness hypotheses, none of which are recorded.',r'The null-vector equations of \citet{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} and localization of \citet{beilinsonschechtman1988determinant} explain the context. They are not evidence for an unconditional finite-rank theorem for every reducible Verma module.',['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','beilinsonschechtman1988determinant'],cls='F',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 finite-rank conformal-block systems exist under substantial additional hypotheses, but this particular unrestricted closing assertion is insufficiently specified.',reconstruction=r'The missing finiteness condition is exposed rather than supplied by silently choosing a minimal model.')
item(694,'P001319-P001321','remark','Publication history and a later correction of the determinant construction',r'The surviving report identifies the Beilinson--Schechtman construction before publication.',r'The 1988 determinant-bundle paper is the direct publication match. A later correction concerns its Theorem 2.3.1(ii), the reduction step under twisting by a divisor etale over the base; this must be distinguished from the general Virasoro-uniformization principle.',r'\citet[Thm. 1.1]{esnaulttsai2000determinant}, circulated October 1999 and published in 2000, repairs the indicated reduction step and gives the corrected comparison. The article was read; it does not justify discarding the 1988 construction as a whole.',['beilinsonschechtman1988determinant','esnaulttsai2000determinant'],cls='A',publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'This bibliographic qualification is editorial context for the fate of the recorded construction, not an additional theorem attributed to the 1986 speaker.')
