talk(695,'1986-11-17','V. A. Kazakov','P001321-P001326','Random surfaces, Gaussian embeddings, and coupled matrix integrals',r'The actual section is P001322--P001325, jointly headed Kazakov and Zakharevich; interventions by Gelfand are marked. It compares the bosonic-string action with triangulated Gaussian surfaces, revisits weighted planar-map counting, and presents a two-source unitary integral and three-state matrix model. The final three- and four-dimensional gauge question repeats record 688. The lattice-theory report beginning P001326 belongs to record 696.')
item(695,'P001322','construction','Bosonic action and Gaussian surfaces on triangulations','The continuum expression integrates \\(X:S\\to\\mathbb R^n\\) and a metric \\(g\\), then replaces it by a sum over triangulations with an edge energy \\(\\sum_{i\\sim j}|x_i-x_j|^2\\).',r'For a fixed finite connected triangulation, assign target vectors to vertices and a positive quadratic graph energy. A random-surface model sums the resulting Gaussian integrals over a specified map class with area and symmetry weights. The formal continuum Polyakov integral additionally needs a measure, gauge fixing, and treatment of zero modes; the displayed discretization is not a proved equality of continuum measures.',r'\citet{polyakov1981quantum} gives the continuum motivation. \citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces}, circulated as PR-1123 in September 1986 and published 29 December 1986, analyzes this dynamical-triangulation model.',['polyakov1981quantum','boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],cls='A',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The graph class, weights, and distinction between a finite Gaussian model and a formal metric integral are supplied.')
item(695,'P001322-P001323','claim','Vertex area weights are not curvature defects','The notes set \\(\\sigma_i=n_i/3\\), call it a defect, and explain it by dividing each regular triangle among its three vertices.','If each equilateral triangle has unit area and \\(n_i\\) triangles meet at vertex \\(i\\), its barycentric area allocation is \\(n_i/3\\), and summing these allocations counts faces. The angular curvature defect is instead \\(2\\pi-n_i\\pi/3\\) at an interior vertex. The two quantities encode different geometric data.','The discrete area and curvature observables are distinguished in \\citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces}. \\ours\\ Counting triangle--vertex incidences gives the area identity and directly explains the notebook diagram; it does not turn \\(n_i/3\\) into an angle defect.',['boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The elementary incidence and angle calculation is an editorial correction, explicitly marked as ours.')
item(695,'P001322-P001323','claim','Piecewise affine Dirichlet energy and the equilateral restriction',r'The integrands are said to agree for regular embeddings, and a fixed metric with a linear map on each triangle is proposed.','For a piecewise affine map on a Euclidean triangulated surface, its Dirichlet energy is a sum of edge squared differences weighted by cotangents of the opposite angles, up to the chosen factor \\(1/2\\). Equilateral triangles make these weights constant. For an arbitrary intrinsic triangulation the unweighted graph energy is a different discretization; target images being equilateral is not the necessary intrinsic-metric condition.',r'The dynamical-triangulation model of \citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces} fixes regular intrinsic triangles. The statement in the notes is a normalization-level discretization observation, not a theorem of convergence to the continuum path integral.',['boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],publication_match='partial',verification='indirect',reconstruction=r'The intrinsic domain metric is separated from the shape of its target image; the edge-energy constant must be fixed before claiming equality.')
item(695,'P001323','question','Gauge topology does not require abandoning cubic lattices',r'Gelfand asks how to write Yang--Mills when characteristic classes appear, saying that one cannot restrict to a cubic lattice.',r'The relevant precise issue is a geometric definition of topological charge for lattice gauge fields with a controlled continuum limit. A hypercubic cellulation does not preclude such a definition: admissibility or small-curvature restrictions can separate topological sectors. The notebook does not specify a different proposed characteristic-class discretization.','\\citet{luscher1982topology}, circulated June 1981 and published in 1982, constructs an integer topological charge for sufficiently small-action-density \\(SU(2)\\) fields on a four-dimensional hypercubic lattice, invariant under continuous admissible deformations and converging to the continuum Chern number. Thus the claimed obstruction to cubic lattices is too strong.',['luscher1982topology'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The topological-charge formulation is the narrowest concrete interpretation supported by the intervention; it is not an invented general open gauge problem.')
item(695,'P001323','claim','High-dimensional concentration does not make every graph triangle equilateral',r'Zakharevich says a random embedding of any triangulated sphere makes all its triangles regular on average, appealing to concentration near a shell.',r'Independent identically distributed isotropic vertex vectors can have pair distances concentrated at a common scale in large target dimension. Coordinates distributed by a graph Gaussian, however, have edge variances determined by the graph covariance and effective resistances; those variances need not be equal. A shell-concentration slogan alone therefore does not justify the asserted equilateral replacement.',r'\citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces} studies limiting target dimensions, but its verified abstract does not supply this universal equilateral theorem. No probability law, limit, or error estimate is stated in the notebook.',['boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],cls='F',speaker='I. S. Zakharevich',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 concentration statements are available for specified distributions; this unrestricted heuristic is not an identified theorem.',reconstruction=r'The two different random-embedding laws are distinguished editorially; no new asymptotic result is claimed.')
item(695,'P001323','method','Target-dimension factorization and removal of translation modes','The \\(n\\)-dimensional embedding integral is written as the \\(n\\)-th power of a one-dimensional Gaussian integral; the parameter \\(n\\) is then continued analytically.','Fix one vertex of a connected graph and write its scalar energy as \\(x^{\\mathsf T}L_*x\\), with positive reduced Laplacian \\(L_*\\). The scalar integral is \\(\\pi^{(v-1)/2}(\\det L_*)^{-1/2}\\), and \\(n\\) independent coordinates give its \\(n\\)-th power for integer \\(n\\ge0\\). The positive determinant permits a specified entire exponential in complex \\(n\\) for each fixed graph. Analyticity of an infinite map sum requires separate convergence control.','The Gaussian-embedding model is the one analyzed by \\citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces}. \\ours\\ Diagonalizing \\(L_*\\) gives the displayed finite Gaussian factor and exposes the missing translational zero-mode removal.',['boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',speaker='I. S. Zakharevich',reconstruction=r'The pinned vertex and measure normalization are explicit editorial repairs; the unpinned integral is infinite.')
item(695,'P001323','claim','The solvable dimensions zero and minus two','Kazakov says the sum can be calculated at \\(n=0\\) and \\(n=-2\\).','At \\(n=0\\) the matter determinant disappears, leaving weighted pure planar maps. At \\(n=-2\\), after fixing the Gaussian normalization, the weight contains \\(\\det L_*\\); the matrix-tree theorem identifies this determinant with the number of spanning trees of the graph. Negative target dimension is determinant analytic continuation, not a Euclidean embedding in a negative-dimensional space.','Pure-map solvability is explicit in \\citet[Sec. 4]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar}. \\citet{boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces} identifies \\(D=-2\\) as exactly solvable and reports its logarithmic mean-square-size behavior. The article appeared in December 1986 after September preprint circulation.',['brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar','boulatovkazakovkostovmigdal1986surfaces'],cls='A',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction=r'The determinant interpretation supplies the precise meaning of the formal negative dimension; the word only is retained as a 1986 contextual remark, not an eternal no-go theorem.')
item(695,'P001323-P001324','question','Rational map weights and the apparent noninteger counting formula','The cubic series is first called an unlabelled count. At \\(k=100\\), \\(72^{100}149!/(102!50!)\\) is observed not to be an integer. Zakharevich replaces the count by a labelled count divided by \\(n!\\).','Connected matrix free energies enumerate maps with inverse-automorphism weights, so rational coefficients are expected. For a labelling set on which the combinatorial automorphism action is faithful, the orbit--stabilizer relation gives \\(\\#\\{\\text{labelled maps}\\}/n!=\\sum_{[T]}1/|\\operatorname{Aut}T|\\). The exact labels must include the flag or cyclic-order data used by the Feynman model. The negative sign in the repeated series belongs to the source convention for negative free energy, not a positive counting function.','\\citet[Secs. 2 and 4]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} gives the graph weights and cubic series. \\oursComputed\\ The 5-adic valuation of the displayed \\(k=100\\) rational number is \\(-1\\), confirming its nonintegrality; the exact check is saved with the chapter evidence.',['brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',speaker='V. A. Kazakov and I. S. Zakharevich',reconstruction=r'The weighting and sign conventions repair the count; triangle labels alone are not asserted to remove every possible residual automorphism.')
item(695,'P001324','claim','Critical exponents survive only regular changes of coupling','The notes transfer a \\(5/2\\) singularity in \\(b\\) to one in \\(\\beta\\), although \\(b=b(\\beta)\\) is not evaluated; the coefficient power is \\(-7/2\\).','The singular exponent transfers unchanged if \\(b(\\beta)\\) is analytic near criticality and \\(b^{\\prime}(\\beta_*)\\ne0\\). If its first nonzero derivative has order \\(m\\), the composed singularity instead has power \\(5m/2\\). The coefficient asymptotic \\(k^{-7/2}\\) pertains to the weighted unrooted pure-map series, with its parity convention, and includes an exponential growth factor.','\\citet[Sec. 4]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} establishes the cubic singularity. \\ours\\ Substituting the first nonzero term of the Taylor expansion of \\(b-b_*\\) gives the necessary change-of-variable qualification.',['brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar'],publication_match='partial',reconstruction='Analytic background terms and the nonvanishing derivative condition are supplied; the note does not determine \\(b(\\beta)\\).',development='annotated')
item(695,'P001324','method','Normalized planar logarithm and higher-valency map potentials','The displayed generating function is \\(Z=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\log I_N\\); the potential may be extended from cubic to cubic plus quartic.','The connected planar free energy is the formal limit \\(\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}N^{-2}\\log[I_N(g,\\lambda)/I_N(0,0)]\\), with a fixed Gaussian normalization. A term \\(\\operatorname{tr}M^k\\) inserts a vertex of valency \\(k\\) in the dual ribbon graph. Cubic and quartic terms therefore mix triangle and quadrilateral faces in the dual map. A positive stabilizing leading coefficient gives a convergent real integral; an unstable cubic potential alone remains formal.',r'\citet[Secs. 2--4]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} treats these matrix integrals and the planar expansion. This repeats 1986-682.2--.3, with an additional quartic coupling.',['brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='The factor \\(N^{-2}\\), Gaussian denominator, and potential sign conditions repair the displayed limit.')
item(695,'P001325','question','Two diagonal source terms and the principal chiral model','Equation (12) has \\(\\operatorname{tr}(p_1U^{-1}p_1U)+\\operatorname{tr}(p_2U^{-1}p_2U)\\), with diagonal \\(p_1,p_2\\), and is identified with a two-dimensional principal chiral field at large \\(N\\).','For finite \\(N\\) and specified source matrices the compact integral is entire in the couplings, and its Taylor coefficients are exactly given by unitary Weingarten calculus. A planar chiral-field equivalence additionally needs a prescribed source eigenvalue distribution, scaling, and a justified reduction or quenching procedure. Exact field-theory information does not automatically evaluate the unspecified finite integral.','\\citet[Cor. 2.4]{collinssniady2006integration} supplies a convergent finite-\\(N\\) coefficient method. This is the same two-source integral family as 1986-688.5 and GP-1986-60; the chapter development gives explicit bounded coefficients and a remainder estimate.',['collinssniady2006integration'],cls='B',problem='GP-1986-60',publication_match='partial',development='developed in this chapter',reconstruction=r'The distinction between an exact compact integral and a continuum planar equivalence is editorial; the latter is not assigned an artificial solved status.',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 exact finite-dimensional coefficient evaluation is established; the notebook does not specify a unique continuum reduction theorem.')
item(695,'P001325','construction','Three-state spins and a three-matrix potential','Equation (13) uses three Hermitian matrices, symmetric pair couplings, and quartic self-interactions, with \\(Z_3\\) spins said to replace Ising spins.',r'Three matrix species with equal unlike-species covariance encode a three-state Potts interaction on the corresponding ribbon graphs. A quartic vertex generates four-valent dual graphs, so calling the same model a sum of purely triangular dual faces needs correction or a duality convention. With the printed sign, a positive quartic coupling destabilizes the real integral; the Feynman expansion is formal.','\\citet[Sec. 1]{daul1995potts}, written November 1994 and posted February 1995, treats the \\(q\\)-state random-planar Potts matrix model and computes three-state scaling information. Its displayed potential is cubic; it is not an exact all-coupling evaluation of the notebook quartic integral.',['daul1995potts'],cls='B',publication_match='partial',literal_correctness='false as written',reconstruction=r'The Potts interpretation, graph valency, and formal-integral qualification prevent a false exact publication match.')
item(695,'P001325','claim','Angular variables around a three-matrix cycle are constrained','After diagonalization, equation (14) integrates three pairwise angular matrices independently and its final factor is again \\(\\mu\\).','If \\(M_i=V_i\\Lambda_iV_i^*\\), set \\(U_{12}=V_1^*V_2\\), \\(U_{23}=V_2^*V_3\\), \\(U_{31}=V_3^*V_1\\). Then \\(U_{12}U_{23}U_{31}=I\\); only two are independent after removing common conjugation. The third spectral pair is \\(\\nu,\\lambda\\), not a second \\(\\nu,\\mu\\) pair. A product of three independent HCIZ integrals therefore gives a different model.',r'\citet[Sec. 1, discussion preceding Eq. (3)]{daul1995potts} explicitly identifies the lack of independence of cyclic relative angles and introduces an auxiliary Gaussian matrix to remove the coupling obstruction. \ours\ The displayed closure identity follows by multiplying the three relative matrices.',['daul1995potts'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='The shared-unitary constraint and final eigenvalue factor are repaired; the scan confirms the repeated \\(\\mu\\) is the literal reading.')
item(695,'P001325','problem','The higher-dimensional gauge integral recurs','The one-site unitary plaquette integral is repeated; \\(d=2\\) is called solved and \\(d=3,4\\) are requested.','The problem is the nonperturbative planar free energy and Wilson-loop observables of a fixed \\(d\\)-matrix plaquette model in dimensions three and four, together with a justified center-symmetric volume-reduction regime. The printed action also needs a real part, or an explicit sum over both plaquette orientations, for the usual real Euclidean gauge weight.',r'\citet{eguchikawai1982reduction} is the original reduction; \citet[Secs. 1--2]{garciaperez2020largeN} explains the center-symmetry obstruction and modern variants. This is a repeat of GP-1986-31, not a new independent problem.',['eguchikawai1982reduction','garciaperez2020largeN'],cls='C',problem='GP-1986-31',publication_match='partial',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',worth=r'Worth a bounded coefficient or symmetry calculation for a specified model; the unrestricted all-coupling higher-dimensional planar solution is a much larger target.',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the general three- and four-dimensional analytic planar solution is not supplied by the established reduction methods; the original weak-coupling four-dimensional untwisted equivalence fails.',reconstruction=r'The phase, orientation convention, and symmetry conditions are needed to formulate the literal target accurately.')
