talk(696,'1986-11-17','I. M. Gelfand','P001324-P001329','Subspace configurations and preprojective reductions of modular lattices',r'The manuscript section P001326--P001327 names a joint project with B. V. Lidskii and V. A. Ponomarev. It reviews subspace configurations and the free modular lattice, distinguishes word equality from linear equivalence, and announces a preprojective quotient and perfect elements. The printed 1987 paper supplies necessary restrictions omitted in the notebook. Finite axiomatizability, decidability, and classification of representations are different questions.')
item(696,'P001326','remark','Projective geometries and continuous geometries as lattice antecedents',r'Gelfand lists Dedekind, Weyl on projective axioms, and von Neumann on factors; dimension is described by lengths of chains, with Desargues in dimension at least three.','The lattice of subspaces of an \\(n\\)-dimensional vector space is complemented and modular with maximal strict-chain length \\(n\\). Projective dimension is \\(n-1\\). Incidence projective spaces of projective dimension at least three are Desarguesian; arbitrary projective planes need not be. Von Neumann continuous geometries replace finite rank by a continuous dimension theory under additional lattice-completeness conditions.',r'The coordinatization framework is developed and precisely recalled in \citet[Introduction and Sec. 2]{herrmann1984arithmetic}. The notebook compresses several classical theories and does not specify the separate work attributed to the uncertain name Disona.',['herrmann1984arithmetic'],publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'The vector-space/projective-dimension shift and the distinction between finite projective geometry and continuous geometry are made explicit.')
item(696,'P001326','theorem','Two and three subspaces: finite indecomposable types',r'Two subspaces are called trivial; three decompose into one-dimensional pieces and a configuration of three lines. The associated free modular lattice has 28 elements.',r'An ordered triple of subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space over a field decomposes into one-dimensional blocks, each subspace being zero or the entire line, and copies of the two-dimensional block of three distinct lines. The decomposition is unique up to isomorphism and order of blocks. Separately, the abstract free modular lattice on three generators has 28 elements; this counts lattice terms modulo modular identities, not indecomposable representation types.',r'\citet[Opening paragraph]{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples} recalls the elementary two- and three-subspace cases. \citet[p. 513]{herrmann1983four} confirms the 28-element free modular lattice. These facts long precede the talk.',['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples','herrmann1983four'],reconstruction=r'The field, ordering, finite dimension, and difference between lattice-element and representation counts are supplied.')
item(696,'P001326','theorem','Four-subspace classification and its parameter families',r'The four-subspace problem is attributed to long work of Gelfand and Ponomarev, with a reference to Manin.',r'Classify ordered quadruples of subspaces of a finite-dimensional vector space up to a common linear isomorphism. The category admits direct-sum decomposition into indecomposables and an explicit classification containing discrete families and regular one-parameter data. Over a general field, irreducible polynomial and rational-canonical data replace eigenvalue parameters; the classification is not a finite list of configurations.',r'\citet{gelfandponomarev1971quadruples}, received 21 October 1970, gives the matching canonical classification; it was already reported in earlier seminar record 1970-198. The 1986 passage is retrospective.',['gelfandponomarev1971quadruples'],reconstruction=r'Field-dependent regular parameters are retained, rather than assuming an algebraically closed field without saying so.')
item(696,'P001326','definition','The free modular lattice and its linear representations','Terms in \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_r\\) use sum and intersection, with equality by axioms; a representation preserves both operations.','Let \\(D^r\\) be the free lattice in the variety of modular lattices on \\(r\\) generators. A finite-dimensional linear representation over \\(k\\) is a lattice homomorphism \\(\\rho:D^r\\to L(V)\\), with \\(L(V)\\) the subspace lattice. Thus a representation is exactly a configuration of \\(r\\) ordered subspaces of \\(V\\), extended to all lattice terms. Abstract equality in \\(D^r\\) need not be the same relation as equality in every such representation.',r'\citet[Sec. 1, pp. 524--525]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} states these definitions. The project builds on \citet{gelfandponomarev1974modular}.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective','gelfandponomarev1974modular'],literal_correctness='correct',reconstruction=r'The field and finite-dimensional representation category are specified.')
item(696,'P001326','theorem','Undecidable equality in free modular lattices','The notes say the word-identity problem is undecidable for \\(r\\ge5\\).','There is no algorithm deciding whether two lattice terms are equal in the free modular lattice on \\(r\\) generators when \\(r\\ge4\\). The result concerns equality modulo all modular identities, not equality in all finite-dimensional subspace lattices over a fixed field.','\\citet[Thm. 1, p. 513]{herrmann1983four} proves the four-generator case and recalls Freese\\textquotesingle s earlier \\(r\\ge5\\) theorem. The note is correct but gives a nonoptimal threshold already in 1986. \\citet[Theorem]{ryzhikova1987decidability} establishes the contrasting decidability of subspace-lattice identities, submitted 16 May 1986 and published in 1987.',['herrmann1983four','ryzhikova1987decidability'],literal_correctness='correct',reconstruction=r'The distinct decision problems are separated; Ryzhikova\textquotesingle s theorem is not a contradiction of the free-modular-lattice result.')
item(696,'P001326','problem','Finite equational axiomatization of linear equivalence',r'Ryzhikova, Semenov\textquotesingle s diploma student, is associated with the question whether linear equivalence can be formulated by finitely many axioms.',r'In the equational interpretation, ask whether all identities valid in subspace lattices of finite-dimensional rational vector spaces follow from finitely many lattice identities. Equivalently, is the lattice variety generated by these subspace lattices finitely based? The analogous class including all fields contains the rational case and is also generated by finite-dimensional lattices. The notebook does not fix a field or say whether axioms must be equations.',r'\citet[Thm. 1.4]{herrmann1984arithmetic}, received February 1982 and revised August 1983, proves that a modular variety containing all rational projective geometries cannot both be finitely based and be generated by its finite-dimensional members. This gives a negative answer to the stated standard interpretation. \citet{ryzhikova1987decidability} answers a different question, algorithmic recognition, affirmatively.',['herrmann1984arithmetic','ryzhikova1987decidability'],problem='GP-1986-35',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the finite equational basis question under the rational/all-field interpretation has a pre-1986 negative answer. Fixed positive-characteristic variants or unrestricted first-order axioms are not silently identified with this formulation.',reconstruction=r'The equational and field quantifiers are explicit editorial specifications. This is not the earlier decidability problem GP-1985-20.')
item(696,'P001326','definition','Morphisms and indecomposability of lattice representations','A morphism \\(V_1\\to V_2\\) takes every represented subspace into its counterpart; indecomposability is introduced through direct sums.','For representations \\(\\rho_i:D^r\\to L(V_i)\\), define \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(\\rho_1,\\rho_2)=\\{f\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_k(V_1,V_2):f(\\rho_1(x))\\subseteq\\rho_2(x)\\ \\forall x\\in D^r\\}\\). It suffices to impose the inclusions on the generators. A direct-sum splitting must split all represented subspaces simultaneously; indecomposable means no nontrivial such splitting.',r'\citet[Sec. 1, p. 525]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} gives exactly this category. It is the standard category underlying the earlier subspace-configuration work.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],literal_correctness='correct',reconstruction=r'The requirement of simultaneous splitting prevents confusion with arbitrary decompositions of the ambient vector space.')
item(696,'P001326-P001327','definition','Preprojective means finiteness of source types, not finiteness of morphisms','The page break gives a definition ending with only finitely many morphisms \\(\\tau:\\rho\\to\\rho_0\\).','A representation \\(\\rho_0\\) is preprojective in this finite-dimensional subspace category when only finitely many isomorphism classes of indecomposable \\(\\rho\\) have \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(\\rho,\\rho_0)\\ne0\\). Over an infinite field every nonzero Hom space itself has infinitely many scalar multiples; counting individual morphisms would therefore destroy the intended definition. Arnold\\textquotesingle s phrase without parameters is an intuition, not an equivalent categorical definition.',r'\citet[Sec. 1, definition and Prop. 1]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} supplies the intended representation-type finiteness. The actual scan confirms the notebook says morphisms, so the repair is mathematical rather than paleographic.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The fixed target, indecomposable source, nonzero Hom condition, and isomorphism-class quotient are supplied.')
item(696,'P001327','theorem','Explicit classification of preprojective representations','Theorem 1 says all preprojective representations of a free lattice on an uncertain \\(r\\) or (4) generators are described.','For the free modular lattice \\(D^r\\) in the range considered by the published paper, the indecomposable preprojective representations consist of the zero-valued one-dimensional representation \\(\\rho_{0,0}\\) and explicitly indexed representations \\(\\rho_{t,l}\\) constructed from homogeneous pieces of a graded algebra \\(A_k^r\\). The algebraic construction works over a specified field \\(k\\); it does not classify all indecomposable \\(r\\)-subspace systems.',r'\citet[Sec. 2, Prop. 6]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective}, received 9 December 1986 and published in 1987, gives the exact classification and points to earlier Gelfand--Ponomarev constructions. This is a direct publication match to the announced description.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],cls='A',publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'The uncertain handwritten generator count is not forcibly read as four. The modern statement specifies the preprojective subcategory and cites the explicit algebraic index rather than claiming a full wild-type classification.')
item(696,'P001327','construction','The preprojective quotient and its representation category',r'Identify lattice elements when every preprojective representation has the same value. Theorem 2 says the preprojective representations of the lattice and its reduction coincide.','Over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), define \\(x\\sim_{\\mathrm{pp}}y\\) when \\(\\rho(x)=\\rho(y)\\) for every indecomposable preprojective representation, and let \\(P_{\\mathbb Q}D^r=D^r/\\sim_{\\mathrm{pp}}\\). The equivalence is a lattice congruence. The published classification of all indecomposable representations of this quotient consists of the original preprojective representations together with the dual all-full one-dimensional representation \\(\\rho_{0,0}^{\\prime}\\). Thus a statement about all quotient representations needs this extra object.',r'\citet[Sec. 1, definition and Prop. 5]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} gives this precise quotient statement. The notebook\textquotesingle s narrower assertion about preprojectives is compatible with it, but does not itself state the all-representation classification.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],cls='A',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'The rational ground field and the distinction between preprojective representations and all quotient representations are supplied.')
item(696,'P001327','problem','Describe the lattice obtained by preprojective reduction',r'The problem of finding the reduction is marked will be done and then actually already done.','For \\(D^r\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), describe exactly which families \\(U_\\rho\\subset V_\\rho\\), indexed by nontrivial indecomposable preprojectives, arise as \\(U_\\rho=\\rho(x)\\) for one lattice element \\(x\\). In the large-rank range of the published theorem, the criterion is compatibility \\(\\sum_{\\tau<\\rho}U_\\tau\\operatorname{Hom}(\\tau,\\rho)\\subseteq U_\\rho\\), with equality for all but finitely many \\(\\rho\\). Equivalently the quotient is described by finitely generated positive-degree homogeneous right ideals of the explicit graded algebra.','\\citet[Thms. 1A--1C]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} publishes the announced description in 1987. The rank condition is essential: its following remark says the assertion is false at \\(r=4\\) and had not been proved there for \\(r=5\\). We use the criterion only for \\(r\\ge7\\), a safe subrange of the OCR-rendered condition \\(r>6\\); the precise endpoint requires a cleaner scan of the paper.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],cls='A',problem='GP-1986-36',publication_match='partial',confidence='medium',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the published large-rank rational description is established. The 1987 rank-five gap is a historical limitation of that theorem, not evidence that a rank-five problem remains open today.',reconstruction=r'The family-compatibility criterion is reconstructed from the published theorem; the notebook contains only the announcement. The conservative rank subrange avoids silently guessing an OCR inequality.')
item(696,'P001327','definition','Perfect elements are tested on indecomposable representations','A perfect element is defined by \\(\\rho(a)=0\\) or \\(V\\) for every representation, and compared to the center of a group ring.','The standard definition requires \\(\\rho(a)\\in\\{0,V\\}\\) for every indecomposable finite-dimensional representation, over the fields under consideration. On a decomposable representation different summands may take different alternatives, so the image can be a proper nonzero direct summand. The analogy with central idempotents concerns such block selection; it is not an isomorphism of lattice and ring centers.',r'\citet{gelfandponomarev1974modular} introduced these perfect elements. \citet[Sec. 1, Prop. 3 and following discussion]{gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective} restates the indecomposable qualifier and the construction from suitable upward-closed subsets of preprojectives.',['gelfandponomarev1974modular','gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The omitted indecomposable restriction is a substantive correction confirmed against the original P001327 scan.')
problem('GP-1986-35','Finite equational basis for subspace-lattice identities',r'Do identities valid in subspace lattices of all finite-dimensional rational vector spaces follow from finitely many lattice identities? This is the explicit equational reading of the notebook question about linear equivalence.',['1986-696.6'],r'As of 12 November 2026 the stated rational formulation and the analogous all-field variety have a negative answer by Herrmann (1984), Theorem 1.4. The field and type of axioms were not stated in the notebook; the decidability problem GP-1985-20 is different.',['herrmann1984arithmetic','ryzhikova1987decidability'])
problem('GP-1986-36','Preprojective reduction of the free modular lattice','Describe the quotient of \\(D^r\\) by equality in all indecomposable preprojective rational representations and characterize its represented subspace families.',['1986-696.11'],r'As of 12 November 2026 the 1987 Gelfand--Lidskii--Ponomarev theorem gives a precise large-rank answer through compatible eventually generated families and graded right ideals. The present chapter uses the safely verified subrange r>=7; it does not turn the historical r=5 exception into a present-day open claim.',['gelfandlidskiiponomarev1987preprojective'])
corr('P001327','Preprojective definition','Only finitely many morphisms rho to rho_0','Only finitely many isomorphism classes of indecomposable rho have Hom(rho,rho_0) nonzero.','Original scan explicitly says morphisms; compare the representation-type finiteness in Gelfand--Lidskii--Ponomarev (1987), Sec. 1.')
corr('P001327','Perfect element definition','For every representation rho, rho(a)=V or 0','For every indecomposable representation rho, rho(a)=V or 0.','Original scan omits indecomposable here; the 1974 and 1987 published definitions require it.')
save()

talk(697,'1986-11-24','A. V. Zelevinsky','P001327-P001331','The unitary dual of general linear groups',r'The talk begins on P001328 and ends at the top of P001330, before Kazakov. It reports the unitary-dual classification of Tadić and Vogan through determinant characters, complementary series, and normalized parabolic induction, then discusses square-integrable building blocks over local fields. Gelfand adds a kernel model for invariant Hermitian forms. The main formulas are recoverable, but the absolute-value normalization, modular character, center quotient, and segment exponents need correction or qualification.')
item(697,'P001328','construction','Unitary characters of the complex multiplicative group','\\(\\delta(z)=(z/|z|)^k|z|^{i\\rho}\\), \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\rho\\in\\mathbb R\\), and \\(u(\\delta,n)=\\delta\\circ\\det\\).','Every continuous unitary character of \\(\\mathbb C^\\times\\) has the displayed form, using the ordinary complex modulus. Composing with determinant gives a one-dimensional unitary representation of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb C)\\). These are the \\(u(\\delta,n)\\) building blocks in the complex-field classification because square-integrable representations modulo center occur here only for \\(\\operatorname{GL}_1(\\mathbb C)\\).',r'\citet[Secs. 1.2 and 9.1]{badulescurenard2010unitary} explicitly gives these blocks and places them in the established Tadić--Vogan classification. The characters themselves are classical.',['badulescurenard2010unitary'],literal_correctness='correct',reconstruction=r'Continuity and the ordinary-modulus convention are specified.')
item(697,'P001328','construction','Stein complementary-series blocks and the complex absolute value','The second family is induced from \\(|\\det|^\\alpha u(\\delta,n)\\otimes|\\det|^{-\\alpha}u(\\delta,n)\\), with \\(0<\\alpha<1/2\\).','Let \\(\\nu(g)=|\\det g|_{\\mathbb C}=|\\det g|^2\\), where the rightmost modulus is ordinary. With normalized parabolic induction, \\(\\pi(u,\\alpha)=\\nu^\\alpha u\\times\\nu^{-\\alpha}u\\) is irreducible and unitarizable for \\(0<\\alpha<1/2\\). If ordinary modulus is used directly, the corresponding exponent interval is \\(0<a<1\\). The completion uses an invariant positive form; the naively twisted inducing characters are not themselves unitary.',r'\citet[Sec. 1.2 and Thm. 7.2]{badulescurenard2010unitary} states the precise complex normalization. \citet{vogan1986unitary}, published October 1986, establishes the surrounding Archimedean classification before this talk.',['badulescurenard2010unitary','vogan1986unitary'],development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The normalized local absolute value and the modular factor in induction are indispensable; the notebook uses the same bars as in its ordinary-modulus character formula without defining the change.')
item(697,'P001328-P001329','definition','Induction, modular normalization, and the relevant adjunction','Gelfand defines induced functions by \\(f(hg)=\\pi(h)f(g)\\), then says restriction and induction are dual functors.','For parabolic \\(P=MN\\) in a reductive local group, normalized smooth induction uses an equivariance factor \\(\\delta_P^{1/2}\\) in addition to the inducing \\(M\\)-representation, with a consistent left/right convention. In the non-Archimedean smooth category its Frobenius adjunction involves the normalized Jacquet functor, not merely ordinary restriction to \\(M\\). For finite groups ordinary induction and restriction satisfy the familiar Frobenius reciprocity. Function spaces and completions matter for locally compact groups.',r'\citet[Sec. 1]{tadic1986classification} and \citet[Secs. 2.4--3]{badulescurenard2010unitary} fix the normalized parabolic conventions used in the classification. The notebook gives only the underlying equivariant-function picture.',['tadic1986classification','badulescurenard2010unitary'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'The categorical and analytic qualifications distinguish an informal induction definition from the precise parabolic functor.')
item(697,'P001329','theorem','Irreducibility, exhaustiveness, and uniqueness of the complex unitary building blocks',r'Theorem (5) says all products of the two block types are irreducible and unitary, every irreducible unitary representation occurs, and equivalence means permutation of blocks.','For \\(\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb C)\\), take a finite multiset of determinant-character blocks \\(u(\\delta,m)\\) and complementary blocks \\(\\pi(u(\\delta,m),\\alpha)\\), \\(0<\\alpha<1/2\\), whose degrees sum to \\(n\\). Their normalized parabolic product is irreducible and unitarizable. Every irreducible unitary representation occurs, and the multiset of blocks is unique. This classifies irreducibles; general unitary representations require direct-integral multiplicity data.',r'\citet{vogan1986unitary} was published in October 1986. The Tadić approach circulated as MPI/SFB 85--22 in 1985; \citet[Secs. 1.2, 7.2 and 12]{badulescurenard2010unitary} gives the precise theorem and later proof history, including the separate ingredients needed for the Archimedean approach.',['vogan1986unitary','badulescurenard2010unitary'],literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction=r'Normalized induction, the local absolute value, and irreducibility rather than arbitrary unitary decompositions are specified.')
item(697,'P001329','definition','Square integrability must be taken modulo the center','\\(D^u\\) is the set of square-integrable representations of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_k(F)\\), with square-integrable matrix coefficients.','The relevant building blocks are irreducible unitary representations whose matrix coefficients are square-integrable on \\(\\operatorname{GL}_k(F)/Z\\), with unitary central character. They need not be square-integrable on the full group, whose center is noncompact. For \\(F=\\mathbb C\\), only \\(k=1\\) occurs; for \\(F=\\mathbb R\\), \\(k=1,2\\) occur; non-Archimedean local fields have higher-degree discrete-series blocks.',r'\citet[Secs. 7 and 9]{badulescurenard2010unitary} uses square integrability modulo center throughout. \citet[Sec. 5]{tadic1986classification} gives the non-Archimedean Langlands ingredients.',['badulescurenard2010unitary','tadic1986classification'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The omitted center quotient is substantive and is restored explicitly.')
item(697,'P001329','construction','Speh blocks as Langlands quotients and the correct segment','\\(u(\\delta,n)\\) is the unique irreducible quotient of an induction with first exponent \\((n-1)/2\\), second \\(n/2-1\\), and final exponent \\(-(n-1)/2\\) without a final \\(\\delta\\).','For an irreducible unitary square-integrable-mod-center representation \\(\\delta\\) of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_k(F)\\), with \\(F\\) a local field, set \\(u(\\delta,n)\\) to be the unique Langlands quotient of \\(\\nu^{(n-1)/2}\\delta\\times\\nu^{(n-3)/2}\\delta\\times\\cdots\\times\\nu^{-(n-1)/2}\\delta\\). Here \\(\\nu=|\\det|_F\\) and the exponents decrease by one. The standard module is generally reducible; its quotient is the block used in the unitary classification.','\\citet[Sec. 7.1, Eqs. (7.1)--(7.3)]{badulescurenard2010unitary} gives the exact formula, with \\(\\nu_\\delta\\) replacing \\(\\nu\\) for general division-algebra inner forms. \\citet[Sec. 5]{tadic1986classification} supplies the split non-Archimedean version.',['badulescurenard2010unitary','tadic1986classification'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='The scan confirms the second exponent \\(n/2-1\\), so replacing it by \\((n-3)/2\\) is a faithful-error correction, not a transcription claim; the last \\(\\delta\\) is restored.')
item(697,'P001329','theorem','The field-uniform classification is a theorem for local fields','The notes say the same theorem remains valid for every field after replacing characters by \\(D^u\\).','For a nondiscrete locally compact field \\(F\\), the split general linear unitary dual is parametrized by normalized products of \\(u(\\delta,n)\\) and \\(\\nu^\\alpha u(\\delta,n)\\times\\nu^{-\\alpha}u(\\delta,n)\\), \\(0<\\alpha<1/2\\), with unique block multisets. The topology, Haar measure, and admissible smooth or Harish-Chandra category are part of the theorem. It is not a theorem about arbitrary abstract fields without topology.',r'\citet[Theorem A and Sec. 7]{tadic1986classification} proves the non-Archimedean local-field result in 1986. \citet{vogan1986unitary} treats Archimedean groups; \citet[Thm. 7.2 and its hypotheses]{badulescurenard2010unitary} unifies the block formulation.',['tadic1986classification','vogan1986unitary','badulescurenard2010unitary'],literal_correctness='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The local-field qualification repairs the phrase every field; finite groups of rational points are a separate representation problem.')
item(697,'P001329','question','Claims about other groups lack a fixed real form','Gelfand asks about other groups. Kirillov lists \\(B_2=C_2\\), \\(G_2\\), \\(SU(2,2)\\), \\(SU(2,n)\\), and names Knapp and Duflo; \\(SO(5)\\sim Sp(4)\\) is written.','A unitary-dual claim must specify the locally compact group, its real or complex form, covering group, and central character conventions. Root-system symbols \\(B_2,C_2,G_2\\) do not supply these data. The relation between orthogonal and symplectic rank-two groups is a Lie-algebra or isogeny relation, not an unqualified isomorphism of the displayed global groups. The note contains no parameter list or theorem for the named non-general-linear groups.',r'\citet[References, Knapp--Speh 1983 and Duflo 1980 entries]{vogan1986unitary} identifies relevant pre-existing classification literature, but this is a thematic lead rather than an exact match to every group in the notebook list. No separate open conjecture is inferred.',['vogan1986unitary'],cls='F',speaker='I. M. Gelfand and A. A. Kirillov',literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',present_status=r'As of 12 November 2026 the notebook list is too compressed to classify each intended real-form theorem responsibly.',reconstruction=r'The absence of a specified real form is retained as a source limitation instead of silently choosing complex groups.')
item(697,'P001329','remark','Quaternionic general linear groups and reducibility normalization',r'The final attribution says Vogan did the quaternionic ones.','The unitary dual of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb H)\\) belongs to the Archimedean general-linear classification. Its analogous block construction uses the reduced norm and a reducibility step \\(\\nu_\\delta\\) attached to each discrete-series block. One cannot copy the split-field exponents with an undefined quaternionic determinant; in particular the reducibility step changes for one-dimensional \\(\\mathbb H^\\times\\) blocks.','\\citet{vogan1986unitary} is the established 1986 classification. \\citet[Prop. 9.10 and Secs. 10--12]{badulescurenard2010unitary} proves the detailed compatibility with the Tadić block description and explains the exceptional step \\(\\nu_\\delta=\\nu^2\\) for one-dimensional \\(\\mathbb H^\\times\\) blocks.',['vogan1986unitary','badulescurenard2010unitary'],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',publication_match='partial',reconstruction=r'The group is inferred from the preceding general-linear context; no claim about all quaternionic classical groups is attributed to the phrase.')
item(697,'P001330','method','Riesz-kernel Hermitian forms and the positive range','Gelfand writes the double integral with kernel \\(|x_1-x_2|^{-\\rho}\\) and says it converges for \\(\\rho<2\\).','On \\(\\mathbb R^2\\simeq\\mathbb C\\), for a Schwartz function \\(f\\) and \\(0<\\rho<2\\), the quadratic form \\(\\iint |x-y|^{-\\rho}f(x)\\overline{f(y)}\\,dx\\,dy\\) is finite and positive. In the Fourier convention \\(\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int e^{-ix\\cdot\\xi}f(x)dx\\), it equals a positive constant times \\(\\int |\\xi|^{\\rho-2}|\\widehat f(\\xi)|^2d\\xi\\). Local kernel integrability requires \\(\\rho<2\\); positivity and decay assumptions are separate issues.','This is the classical homogeneous-distribution mechanism treated in \\citet[Ch. II, Sec. 3]{gelfandshilov1964generalized}; its representation-theoretic application belongs to intertwining-operator theory such as \\citet{knappstein1971intertwining}. \\ours\\ Positivity in the stated range also follows directly by writing \\(|x|^{-\\rho}=\\Gamma(\\rho/2)^{-1}\\int_0^\\infty t^{\\rho/2-1}e^{-t|x|^2}dt\\) and using the positive Fourier transform of each Gaussian.',['gelfandshilov1964generalized','knappstein1971intertwining'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The ambient real dimension two, Schwartz test space, and lower positivity bound are reconstructed from the threshold; the note does not specify its test-function space.')
item(697,'P001330','remark','Analytic continuation alone does not preserve positivity','For \\(\\rho>2\\) the notes demand analytic continuation and say the form will no longer be positive definite; a Fourier-side weighted norm is sketched.','The homogeneous kernel admits meromorphic continuation as a tempered distribution, with poles at even positive values of \\(\\rho\\) in real dimension two. Away from poles, its Fourier coefficient is proportional to \\(2^{2-\\rho}\\pi\\Gamma(1-\\rho/2)/\\Gamma(\\rho/2)\\); its sign depends on the parameter interval and any scalar normalization. Thus continuation is not automatically positive, but the blanket claim of nonpositivity for every \\(\\rho>2\\) is also too strong. Invariant subquotients and moment-vanishing conditions require their own analysis.',r'\citet[Chs. II--III]{gelfandshilov1964generalized} provides the homogeneous-distribution framework; \citet{knappstein1971intertwining} develops the intertwining and complementary-series context. The notebook gives no specific representation whose positivity range can be fully reconstructed.',['gelfandshilov1964generalized','knappstein1971intertwining'],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',development='annotated',reconstruction=r'The unnormalized continuation, its poles, and scalar sign are distinguished from positivity of a specified representation-theoretic quotient.')
corr('P001328','Complementary-series modulus','Ordinary-looking absolute determinant raised to alpha, 0<alpha<1/2','Use normalized local modulus |det|_C=ordinary |det| squared for the interval 0<alpha<1/2.','Badulescu--Renard (2010), Sec. 1.2 explicitly states the square-modulus convention.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P001329','Discrete-series definition','Square-integrable matrix coefficients on GL(k,F)','Square integrability modulo the center, with unitary central character.','The center is noncompact; source definitions in Tadic (1986) and Badulescu--Renard (2010) include the quotient.')
corr('P001329','Speh segment exponents','Second exponent n/2-1; no delta on final factor','Second exponent (n-3)/2; final factor nu^(-(n-1)/2) delta.','Original scan viewed; the overwritten second exponent reads n/2-1, in agreement with both transcriptions. Compare Badulescu--Renard Eq. (7.1).')
corr('P001329','Field scope','For every field','For nondiscrete locally compact fields in the relevant topological representation category.','Theorems of Tadic and Vogan assume local fields and normalized smooth/Harish-Chandra induction.')
save()

